{"id":"y10-1-2-l4","label":"1.2 L4: File Size","steps":[{"id":"do-now","label":"Do Now: Sound Lab Recap","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y10l4-donow","validatorKey":"y10_1_2_l4_donow","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Do Now: Sound Lab Recap</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Parts (a) and (b) are a real Cambridge exam question (0478/13, June 2025, Question 1). Parts (c) and (d) are adapted from the same question.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-donow-a\">(a) State what is meant by the sample rate of a sound recording. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-donow-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"donow-a\" aria-label=\"Do now part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-donow-b\">(b) State what is meant by the sample resolution of a sound recording. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-donow-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"donow-b\" aria-label=\"Do now part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-donow-c\">(c) Give the term for one individual measurement taken during sampling. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-donow-c\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"donow-c\" aria-label=\"Do now part c\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-donow-d\">(d) State one effect on the file size of a recording of increasing its sample resolution. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-donow-d\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"donow-d\" aria-label=\"Do now part d\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10l4-donow-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 4 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10l4-donow-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"},{"id":"title","label":"File Size Calculator","content":"<div class=\"lesson-title-slide\"><p class=\"lesson-title-kicker\">Data Representation</p><h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">File Size Calculator</h2><p>Year 10, 1.2 L4 &middot; File Size &middot; 30 minutes</p></div><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li><strong>Concept:</strong> Turn every bit total from this topic into real storage units.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Drag a character count and watch a text file's size grow live.</li><li><strong>Exam link:</strong> Three real Cambridge questions on the same unit ladder.</li><li><strong>Also today:</strong> Turning a sound recording's bits/sec into a full file size.</li></ul>"},{"id":"concept","label":"Bits, Bytes and Everything Above","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Bits, Bytes and Everything Above</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Every calculation so far this topic has ended in a number of <strong>bits</strong> - characters &times; bits per character, width &times; height &times; colour depth, sample rate &times; sample resolution. Turning that bit total into a file size uses one ladder of units, each one 1024 times bigger than the one below it.</p><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li><strong>1 nibble</strong> = 4 bits (half a byte)</li><li><strong>1 byte</strong> = 8 bits = 2 nibbles</li><li><strong>1 kilobyte (KB / kibibyte, KiB)</strong> = 1024 bytes</li><li><strong>1 megabyte (MB / mebibyte, MiB)</strong> = 1024 KB</li><li><strong>1 gigabyte (GB / gibibyte, GiB)</strong> = 1024 MB</li></ul><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Cambridge exam papers use the exact names KiB, MiB and GiB for this &times;1024 ladder - the same multiplier this course has already used for every file size readout in Pixel Lab and Sound Lab.</p>"},{"id":"exam-units","label":"Exam Question: Unit Conversions","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y10l4-examunits","validatorKey":"y10_1_2_l4_examunits","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Exam Question: Unit Conversions</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Cambridge IGCSE 0478/12, March 2023, Question 3(a) asked students to complete a table of data storage conversions like this one.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examunits-a\">(a) 8 bytes = ................ nibbles</label><input id=\"y10l4-examunits-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examunits-a\" aria-label=\"Exam units part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examunits-b\">(b) 512 kibibytes (KiB) = ................ mebibytes (MiB)</label><input id=\"y10l4-examunits-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examunits-b\" aria-label=\"Exam units part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examunits-c\">(c) 4 gibibytes (GiB) = ................ mebibytes (MiB)</label><input id=\"y10l4-examunits-c\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examunits-c\" aria-label=\"Exam units part c\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examunits-d\">(d) 1 exbibyte (EiB) = ................ pebibytes (PiB)</label><input id=\"y10l4-examunits-d\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examunits-d\" aria-label=\"Exam units part d\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10l4-examunits-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 4 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10l4-examunits-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"},{"id":"challenge-text","label":"Challenge: Text File Size Live","type":"file-size-calculator","containerId":"y10l4-fsize-text","mode":"text","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Challenge: Text File Size, Live</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Drag the character count and choose an encoding. Watch the bar - and the file size underneath it - change in real time, the same way resolution and colour depth changed Pixel Lab's photo.</p><div id=\"y10l4-fsize-text\"></div><p class=\"lesson-lead\" style=\"margin-top:10px\">Notice how much bigger the same message gets once it needs 16-bit Unicode instead of 8-bit ASCII.</p>"},{"id":"exam-image","label":"Exam Question: Bits to Kibibytes","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y10l4-examimage","validatorKey":"y10_1_2_l4_examimage","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Exam Question: Bits to Kibibytes</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Cambridge IGCSE 0478/12, June 2024, Question 1 asked students to work with these units directly, then apply them to an image using the width &times; height &times; colour depth formula from Pixel Lab.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examimage-a\">(a) Give the name of the data storage measurement equal to 8 bits. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-examimage-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examimage-a\" aria-label=\"Exam image part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examimage-b\">(b) State how many bits there are in a kibibyte (KiB). [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-examimage-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examimage-b\" aria-label=\"Exam image part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examimage-c\">(c) Give the name of the data storage measurement equal to 1024 gibibytes (GiB). [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-examimage-c\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examimage-c\" aria-label=\"Exam image part c\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examimage-d\">(d) A 16-bit colour image has a resolution of 512 pixels wide by 512 pixels high. Calculate the file size of the image in kibibytes (KiB). [3]</label><input id=\"y10l4-examimage-d\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examimage-d\" aria-label=\"Exam image part d\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10l4-examimage-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 6 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10l4-examimage-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"},{"id":"exam-image-2","label":"Exam Question: The Same Ladder Again","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y10l4-examimage2","validatorKey":"y10_1_2_l4_examimage2","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Exam Question: The Same Ladder, One More Time</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Cambridge IGCSE 0478/12, March 2026, Question 1 tests the unit ladder and a full image file size calculation together.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><p class=\"lesson-lead\" style=\"margin-top:0\">Which statement is correct?<br>A One bit equals four nibbles.<br>B One byte equals 16 bits.<br>C One exbibyte (EiB) equals 1024 &times; 1024 tebibytes (TiB).<br>D One mebibyte (MiB) equals 1024 &times; 1024 gibibytes (GiB).</p><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examimage2-a\">(a) Which statement (A-D) is correct? [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-examimage2-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examimage2-a\" aria-label=\"Exam image 2 part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-examimage2-b\">(b) An image file has a resolution of 1024 pixels high &times; 1024 pixels wide. The colour depth is two bytes. Calculate the file size of the image. Give the answer in MiB. [2]</label><input id=\"y10l4-examimage2-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"examimage2-b\" aria-label=\"Exam image 2 part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10l4-examimage2-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 3 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10l4-examimage2-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"},{"id":"sound-extension","label":"From Bits/Sec to a Full File","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y10l4-soundext","validatorKey":"y10_1_2_l4_soundext","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">From Bits/Sec to a Full File</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Sound Lab worked out bits per second (sample rate &times; sample resolution). A full recording also has a length - multiply by the duration in seconds to get the whole file's size.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-soundext-a\">A recording lasts 4 seconds, sampled at 20 samples/sec with an 8-bit sample resolution. (a) Calculate the number of bits in the whole recording. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-soundext-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"soundext-a\" aria-label=\"Sound extension part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-soundext-b\">(b) Calculate the file size of the same recording in bytes. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-soundext-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"soundext-b\" aria-label=\"Sound extension part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10l4-soundext-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 2 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10l4-soundext-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"},{"id":"practice","label":"Checked Practice","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y10l4-practice","validatorKey":"y10_1_2_l4_practice","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Checked Practice</h2><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-practice-a\">(a) Calculate the number of bits needed to store a text file of 25 characters using 8-bit extended ASCII. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-practice-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"practice-a\" aria-label=\"Practice part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-practice-b\">(b) State the number of bits in 1 nibble. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-practice-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"practice-b\" aria-label=\"Practice part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-practice-c\">(c) A sound recording lasts 3 seconds, sampled at 10 samples/sec with an 8-bit sample resolution. Calculate the file size of the recording in bytes. [2]</label><input id=\"y10l4-practice-c\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"practice-c\" aria-label=\"Practice part c\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10l4-practice-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 4 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10l4-practice-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"},{"id":"plenary","label":"Plenary: File Size","type":"short-answer-validation","validatorId":"y10l4-plenary","validatorKey":"y10_1_2_l4_plenary","content":"<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Plenary</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Answer from memory - no notes, no going back to the challenges.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-plenary-a\">(a) State what is meant by a kibibyte (KiB). [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-plenary-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-a\" aria-label=\"Plenary part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-plenary-b\">(b) State the number of bits in 1 byte. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-plenary-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-b\" aria-label=\"Plenary part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-plenary-c\">(c) State one reason a text file with more characters has a larger file size. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-plenary-c\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-c\" aria-label=\"Plenary part c\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10l4-plenary-d\">(d) Calculate the file size, in bytes, of a 20-character message stored using 16-bit Unicode. [1]</label><input id=\"y10l4-plenary-d\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-id=\"plenary-d\" aria-label=\"Plenary part d\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10l4-plenary-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 4 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10l4-plenary-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"}],"validators":{"y10_1_2_l4_examunits":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*16(\\s*nibbles?)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"1 byte is 2 nibbles - multiply the byte count by 2."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*0?\\.5(\\s*mib)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Divide by 1024 to move up one step of the ladder."},{"suffix":"c","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*4096(\\s*mib)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Multiply by 1024 to move down one step of the ladder."},{"suffix":"d","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*1024(\\s*pib)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Every step of the ladder is the same ×1024 jump, all the way up to exbibytes."}],"y10_1_2_l4_examimage":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*bytes?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"This is the standard unit made of 8 bits."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*8192(\\s*bits?)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"A kibibyte is 1024 bytes - and each byte is 8 bits."},{"suffix":"c","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"tebibyte|\\btib\\b","flags":"i"},"feedback":"This is the next step up from gibibyte on the same ×1024 ladder."},{"suffix":"d","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*512(\\s*kib)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Multiply width × height × colour depth for bits, divide by 8 for bytes, then by 1024 for KiB."}],"y10_1_2_l4_examimage2":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*c\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Work through each option using the ×1024 ladder - three of them break it."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*2(\\s*mib)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Convert the colour depth to bits first, multiply by the number of pixels, then divide down to MiB in two ×1024 steps."}],"y10_1_2_l4_soundext":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*640(\\s*bits?)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Work out bits per second first (rate × resolution), then multiply by the duration in seconds."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*80(\\s*bytes?)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Take your answer to part (a) and divide by 8."}],"y10_1_2_l4_practice":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*200(\\s*bits?)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Multiply the character count by the bits used for each character."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*4(\\s*bits?)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"A nibble is half a byte."},{"suffix":"c","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*30(\\s*bytes?)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Work out bits per second first (rate × resolution), multiply by the duration, then divide by 8 for bytes."}],"y10_1_2_l4_plenary":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"1024\\s*bytes","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Think about how many bytes make up one KiB - the same ×1024 relationship used throughout this ladder."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*8(\\s*bits?)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"This is the standard number of bits in a byte, used throughout this topic."},{"suffix":"c","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"more (characters|bits|data)|each character (needs|uses|takes)|larger|increase","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Think about what happens to the total number of bits as the character count goes up."},{"suffix":"d","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^\\s*40(\\s*bytes?)?\\s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Multiply the character count by 16 bits per character, then divide by 8."}],"y10_1_2_l4_donow":[{"suffix":"a","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"(number|amount).{0,20}samples.{0,15}(per|each|every|a).{0,10}second|samples.{0,10}(per|each).{0,10}second","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Think about what is being counted, and over what unit of time."},{"suffix":"b","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"(number|amount).{0,15}bits.{0,20}(each|per|store|represent).{0,15}sample|bits.{0,15}(per|used for).{0,10}(eachs+)?sample","flags":"i"},"feedback":"Think about what is being measured for each individual sample, not the recording as a whole."},{"suffix":"c","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"^s*(as+)?samples?s*$","flags":"i"},"feedback":"This is the single measurement itself - the same word Sound Lab used for one point on the reconstruction line."},{"suffix":"d","pattern":{"__regex":true,"source":"larger file|increase.{0,20}(file|storage|data)|more (data|bits)|bigger","flags":"i"},"feedback":"More bits per sample means more data stored for every single measurement - think about what that does to the total."}]},"pseudocodeValidators":{}}